{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And the one did not come near the other all night.</b> At night they began to enter the sea, for what he mentioned in the preceding verse, ״ויסע מלאך האלהים״ — 'and the angel of Elohim journeyed' — was at the beginning of the night; it journeyed, and the intent therein was the attribute of judgment, which is called 'angel' in certain places in Scripture, and therefore it mentioned אלהים. And the Rav, of blessed memory, said that it is possible that it is not contiguous but rather at morning-light. And the matter was that this angel journeyed in the pillar of fire at night from before Israel and went behind them, and the pillar of cloud journeyed further from before them and stood behind them — and behold, both pillars were behind them. And when it was the ashmoret of morning, the Egyptians were judged in the sea; and there is a hint in the word אשמורת, as in its Targum." "<b>And the order</b> in this matter was: the camp of Israel first, and after the camp of Israel, close to them, the pillar of fire, and after the pillar of fire the pillar of cloud, and after the pillar of cloud the camp of Egypt. It follows that the two pillars intervened between the camp of Egypt and the camp of Israel. And it says 'this' — זה — about the pillar of cloud that intervened between the camp of Egypt and the pillar of fire: ״ויהי הענן והחשך בין מחנה למחנה ויאר את הלילה״ — for there was light for Israel from the pillar of fire that was behind them and close to them, since the pillar of cloud did not intervene between them as it did for the Egyptians. ״ולא קרב זה אל זה כל הלילה״ — for it was not possible for one camp to draw near to the other, because the two pillars intervened." "<b>Now behold,</b> the Egyptians who were behind the pillar of cloud could see Israel, for they could see the pillar of fire through the cloud, since one who sits in darkness sees light from afar. But Israel could not see the Egyptians, for one who has light before him does not see one who sits in darkness. And because of this, the pillar of fire, which was a great necessity, had been going before the camp of Israel — it journeyed and stood behind them; for had it gone before them to illuminate for them as on other nights, Israel would have rushed onward and the Egyptians would not have seen them and would not have come after them." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״ולא קרב זה אל זה״ — 'and the one did not come near the other': the supernal beings did not recite song. <b>And in Pirkei</b> de-Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 42: do not read ״ולא קרב זה אל זה״ — 'and the one did not come near the other,' but rather ״ולא קרא זה אל זה״ — 'and the one did not call to the other,' all night — that is, from what is written (Isaiah 6:3): ״וקרא זה אל זה ואמר קדוש״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:06.563000029Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}